---
import Bench from "./Bench.astro";
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import PartHead from "./PartHead.astro";
import NextPart from "./NextPart.astro";
import Beat from "./Beat.astro";
import Touchline from "./Touchline.astro";
import AiStudy from "./AiStudy.astro";
import { boundsPrompt } from "../../lib/primer";
import { bounds, pack, story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { dunmoreSplit, hartwell, pipeline, spell } from "../../lib/cases";

const signed = (n: number) => (n > 0 ? `+${n}` : String(n));
const reach = [
  [`${pipeline.silent} of ${pipeline.asked}`, "schedule pages missing"],
  [`${bounds.unseen} of ${story.finals}`, "finished matches lost with it"],
  [`${pipeline.rows} of ${pipeline.rows}`, "standings rows wrong"],
];
const outcomes = [
  ["Loss", 0],
  ["Draw", 1],
  ["Win", 3],
] as const;
---

<section id="case-hartwell">
  <PartHead id="case-hartwell">Case 2. Hartwell: gaps arrive in blocks</PartHead>
  <Pipeline small lit="build" note="This case happens at Build" />

  <p class="cv-callout">Coach Dolan, Hartwell Tech: “We went into Dunmore October fifteenth and put seven on 'em. Seven-nothing. You've got us fourth.”</p>
  <p>Hartwell's own schedule page answered, yet its row is still wrong.</p>

  <Beat part="case-hartwell" name="Look" title="Why Hartwell's row is wrong" />
  <p>Kick read each match once, from the home team's page, so no match would be counted twice. That {hartwell.seven} win was played at Dunmore.</p>
  <div class="cv-predict" data-once="rows" data-predict>
    <div class="cv-check">
      <p class="cv-kind">Prediction <span>not graded</span></p>
      <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={pipeline.rows} data-why="">
        <label for="cv-rows">Only one schedule page is missing. How many of the {spell(pipeline.rows)} rows in the standings table are wrong?</label>
        <input id="cv-rows" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="q-rows" />
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <p class="cv-once">Record a prediction, then test it in the simulator.</p>
      <button type="button" data-submit>Record prediction and open simulator</button>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>You predicted <b data-show="q-rows"></b>. Friday is set up below. Read the last count first. Then try two things: switch other schools' sites off and back on, and, with only Dunmore's site off, switch how Kick reads each match.</p>
      <Bench id="bench-hartwell" controls={["pages", "rule"]} caption="Friday as it happened." />
      <p>All {spell(pipeline.rows)}. One page wipes out a whole row of the match matrix: Dunmore's {spell(bounds.unseen)} home matches, which are also the other {spell(bounds.unseen)} schools' away matches there. The standings break for teams whose own pages answered.</p>
      <p>When Kick reads both schools' pages, all {spell(bounds.unseen)} lost results come back, because each visitor's saved schedule shows the score. Hartwell Tech moves up to {["first", "second", "third", "fourth"][hartwell.after - 1]}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="skew">
    <p class="cv-kind">Knowledge check</p>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="unknown" data-why="Can't tell from Kick's table. The skew depends on how Dunmore played at home, and Kick's table holds none of those matches. Home advantage is a guess about matches nobody saw." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ high: "That assumes Dunmore conceded fewer at home, as home advantage would suggest. Kick's table has no Dunmore home match to check that against.", low: "True, and Coach Dolan's 7-0 is enough to show it. But that came from a voicemail, from outside the table. Kick's table alone has no Dunmore home match to compare with." })}>
      <label><span>Kick's original table holds all {spell(bounds.played)} of Dunmore's finished away matches and none of its home matches. Using that table alone, which way is Dunmore's goals conceded per match skewed?</span>
        <select data-save="bias-dir"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="low">Too low</option><option value="high">Too high</option><option value="unknown">Can't tell from Kick's table</option></select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Choose one, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answer</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>Here is what the recovered home matches showed.</p>
      <figure class="cv-split">
        <table>
          <caption>How the missing block skews one team's stats</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dunmore</th><th scope="col">Matches</th><th scope="col">Goals conceded / match</th><th scope="col">Goal difference</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">In Kick's table</th><td>{dunmoreSplit.kick.p}</td><td>{dunmoreSplit.kick.perMatch}</td><td>{signed(dunmoreSplit.kick.gd)}</td></tr>
            <tr class="is-true"><th scope="row">Across all {dunmoreSplit.all.p}</th><td>{dunmoreSplit.all.p}</td><td>{dunmoreSplit.all.perMatch}</td><td>{signed(dunmoreSplit.all.gd)}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>Dunmore's defense struggled at home, so a table of away matches only made it look solid. A different team could have leaned the other way.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Beat part="case-hartwell" name="Name it" title="How far one gap reaches" />
  <p class="cv-lede">Gaps arrive in blocks. A single outage wipes out related records together and hits the same teams repeatedly.</p>
  <p>Dunmore's split has a name: <strong>selection bias</strong>. Every match Kick kept was read correctly, but the matches left are not a fair sample of Dunmore's season, because the missing ones were its whole home block. Statisticians also sort gaps by why they happened. <a href="../01-collection-ai-coverage/">Part 2's AI reply</a> covers the three patterns, and the <a href="../01-collection-field-work/?from=case-hartwell#statistics">deep dive</a> tests them on Friday.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="bias">
    <p class="cv-kind">Knowledge check</p>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="venue" data-why="Any Dunmore stat where home and away performance differ. Matches played is simply short, while goals conceded per match is tilted because only away matches were kept." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ none: "Each individual match was parsed accurately, but the subset of matches is skewed.", points: "Points are short, and the next section shows how far. The skew reaches any rate or average where home and away differ." })}>
      <label><span>Which of Dunmore's stats in Kick's table does this missing block skew?</span>
        <select data-save="bias-which"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="none">None, since every match included was read correctly</option><option value="points">Only its points</option><option value="venue">Any stat where home and away performance differ</option></select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Choose one, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answer</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Coach Dolan's {hartwell.seven} already shows which way Dunmore's numbers lean: seven goals conceded at home in one match, against {dunmoreSplit.kick.perMatch} a match away. Only the recovered scores show how far, from {dunmoreSplit.kick.perMatch} to {dunmoreSplit.all.perMatch} goals conceded per match.</p></div>
  </div>

  <p>The gap also reaches further than its size suggests. That's why Part 2's second rule says to count in the unit of analysis, the thing your claim counts. Counted in pages, Kick lost one of {spell(pipeline.asked)}. Counted in matches, it lost {bounds.unseen} of {story.finals}, about the same share. What the matches show is who was hit: every team played at Dunmore, so every row is wrong.</p>
  <ol class="cv-reach" aria-label="How far Dunmore's missing page reaches">
    {reach.map(([n, what]) => <li><b>{n}</b><span>{what}</span></li>)}
  </ol>

  <p>Even before a lost result is recovered, you can limit its uncertainty. Kick's table gives Hartwell Tech {hartwell.pts} points, and the expected set shows one of its finished matches is lost. That match adds 0 for a loss, 1 for a draw or 3 for a win, so Hartwell's true total must lie within {hartwell.pts} and {hartwell.high}. That range is a <strong>bound</strong>: you don't know the exact value, but you know the limits it cannot cross. This bound comes from the count of lost matches and the scoring rules. It says nothing about which value is likely.</p>
  <figure class="cv-bound" aria-label={`Hartwell Tech's possible totals: ${hartwell.pts}, ${hartwell.pts + 1} or ${hartwell.high}`}>
    <p class="cv-bound-head"><b>Hartwell Tech</b><span>{hartwell.pts} points counted, one match lost</span></p>
    <ol>{outcomes.map(([what, add]) => <li><span>{what}</span><b>{hartwell.pts + add}</b><small>+{add}</small></li>)}</ol>
    <figcaption>Bound: {hartwell.pts} to {hartwell.high}. With one match lost, {hartwell.pts + 2} is inside the bound but can't happen.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <AiStudy
    id="hartwell-ai-study"
    title="How far can one gap move a number?"
    note="Send this prompt to read the reply an AI assistant gave, or copy it into your own. Then come back and bound Dunmore's points."
    prompt={boundsPrompt}
    href="../01-collection-ai-bounds/"
  />

  <Touchline part="case-hartwell" at={["Resolve"]} note="At Resolve, where two pages' rows become one match." title="Reading both schools' pages">
    <p>Reading both schools' schedules gives Touchline two chances to find each result. If the visitor's saved schedule shows the score, Touchline can still count the result when the host's page fails. The trade-off is a matching step: the same match, written differently on two sites, has to be recognized as one match and counted once. <a href="../05-identity/">Module 5</a> covers how that works.</p>
  </Touchline>

  <Beat part="case-hartwell" name="Your claim" title="Bound Dunmore's points" />
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="claim-bounds">
    <p class="cv-kind">Apply it</p>
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={`${bounds.points},${bounds.high}`} data-why={`${bounds.points} to ${bounds.high}. If Dunmore lost all ${spell(bounds.unseen)}, it stays at ${bounds.points}. If it won all ${spell(bounds.unseen)}, it gains ${3 * bounds.unseen} points. Its actual total turned out to be ${pack.dunmorePoints}, right inside the bound.`}>
      <p class="cv-qlabel">Kick's table gives Dunmore {bounds.points} points from {spell(bounds.played)} matches, with {spell(bounds.unseen)} finished matches lost. Using only Kick's table, the expected set and the scoring rules, fill in the bound that belongs beside it.</p>
      <p class="cv-row cv-fillin">
        <span>Dunmore: {bounds.points} points so far, truly between</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Lowest possible total" data-save="q-low" />
        <span>and</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Highest possible total" data-save="q-high" />
      </p>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Enter both endpoints, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check bounds</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>Bounds tell readers exactly who is affected and how far each number could move, before a single lost score is recovered.</p>
      <p class="cv-further">Optional deep dive: <a href="../01-collection-field-work/?from=case-hartwell#statistics">Statistics of the gap</a> covers where an expected set comes from, why bounds are linked, and how to test your assistant on the missingness patterns.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <NextPart from="case-hartwell">If every schedule page answers, is the table complete?</NextPart>
</section>
